<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Tall Tree! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Tall Tree!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name primary and secondary colors. Interactive: Express likes or dislikes using simple words and pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify and name colors of familiar nature objects (e.g., "green trees," "pink flowers," or "yellow leaves"). Can respond to simple questions about nature with words or gestures (e.g., "What is this?" Student points and says, "tree."). Can express likes or dislikes about nature-related objects using simple words and pointing (e.g., "I like trees" while pointing to a tree).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large, colorful flashcards of garden objects (tree, flower, leaf, plant, sun). Teacher points to each and asks, "What is this?" encouraging choral repetition. Then, the teacher holds up different colored objects (e.g., a green leaf, a pink flower toy) and asks, "What color is this?" to activate prior knowledge of colors and garden vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a green tree and models, "This is a tree. It is green. I like green trees." The teacher then holds up a flashcard of a pink flower, modeling, "This is a flower. It is pink. I like pink flowers." The teacher uses enthusiastic tone and pointing for "I like." For "I do not like," the teacher shakes their head and says, "I do not like (e.g., brown leaves)," emphasizing simple rejection.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a green tree flashcard: "Is this a blue tree?" (Expected answer: "No!") "Is it green?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a pink flower: "Do I like this flower?" (Teacher smiles and nods as if liking, students mimic or say "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful illustration or picture book depicting a simple garden scene with different plants (tree, flower, bush, leaf) in various colors (green, pink, yellow, brown). The illustration should have clear labels for objects, making it a "narrative excerpt" presented visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about nature: Describe plants and trees with singular phrases (e.g., "The tree is tall." "The flower is pink."). Asking questions in nature: Use basic questions like "What color is the flower?" to learn about plants.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the garden illustration. "Look! It's our garden!" (pointing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a specific object: "This is a tall tree. It is green. What color is the leaf? It is green." (Emphasize /t/ in 'tree' and /g/ in 'garden' and 'green').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a flower: "This is a flower. It is pink. I like pink flowers!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher introduces "What color is this?" question using the picture. Teacher asks and answers for students to hear the model. Example: "What color is the flower?" (Teacher points) "It is pink."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions, Choral Response):</b> The teacher asks simple questions, encouraging whole-class oral answers or pointing.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a garden?" (Yes!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is the tree?" (Green!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is the flower?" (Pink!)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Name primary and secondary colors. Express likes or dislikes.
                    Grammar: Noun + verb + adjective ("The flowers are pink."). WH-questions ("What color is the flower?"). Present simple ("This is a tall tree.").
                    Vocabulary: garden, tree, flower, plant, leaf, pink, green, tall, short, small, big, what, this, I like.
                    Pronunciation: /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree". Phonemic awareness: /s/ in "sun".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Oral Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays a series of flashcards with garden objects (tree, flower, leaf) in specific colors. The teacher starts a sentence and students complete it orally as a whole class or in small groups.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a green tree: "This is a... (tree). It is... (green)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a pink flower: "This is a... (flower). It is... (pink)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a small leaf: "This is a... (leaf). It is... (small)."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "I Like / I Do Not Like" Substitution Drill (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher presents flashcards and prompts students to express likes or dislikes using a sentence frame.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a 'pink flower' flashcard: "I like..." (Students say: "I like pink flowers!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a 'green tree' flashcard: "I like..." (Students say: "I like green trees!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a 'brown dead leaf' flashcard: "I do not like..." (Students say: "I do not like brown leaves!"). Teacher models negative gesture.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Pair Guessing Game ("What Color Is It?"):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small set of colored flashcards (tree, flower, leaf, sun). Student A picks a card secretly and says, "What color is my [flower]?" Student B guesses, "Is it pink?" Student A responds, "Yes, it is pink!" or "No, it is not pink. Guess again!" Then they switch roles. Teacher circulates to monitor pronunciation and accuracy of "What color is...?" and "It is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Pair Talk and Show</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into pairs. Each pair receives a large picture of a simple garden with various plants and colors (e.g., a tall green tree, a small pink flower, a yellow sun, brown soil, green bush).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student A</b> points to an object in their garden picture and asks, "What is this?" or "What color is the [flower]?" (Pragmatic: Asking questions in nature).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student B</b> identifies the object and its color, then expresses a like or dislike. Example: (pointing to a pink flower) "It is a flower. It is pink. I like pink flowers!" (Sociolinguistic: Respect for nature; Pragmatic: Describing plants and expressing likes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns pointing, asking, and answering. Teacher encourages them to use the target vocabulary (garden, tree, flower, plant, leaf, sun, soil, pink, green, tall, short, big, small) and grammar (Noun + verb + adjective, WH-questions, Present simple, demonstratives 'this').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, listens to student interactions, and provides support with vocabulary or pronunciation as needed without interrupting fluency. Students should focus on communicating their ideas.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair briefly presents a part of their "garden talk" to the class or to the teacher individually, demonstrating their ability to ask about and describe garden objects, their colors, and express likes/dislikes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Can pronounce /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree" clearly, and /s/ in "sun".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Accurately names garden objects (tree, flower, leaf) and colors (pink, green, yellow).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Uses "This is a...", "It is [color]", and "I like [object]"/ "I do not like [object]".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Speaks with reasonable pause and without excessive hesitation for their level.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Interaction:</b> Actively participates in asking and answering questions with their partner.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a thumbs up (yes) or thumbs down (no) to answer:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I name colors of plants?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I tell my partner if I like or dislike a plant?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral Prompts):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today about the garden?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part about talking about the garden with your friend?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., pair work engagement, clarity of instructions, use of visuals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was challenging for the students or required additional scaffolding? (e.g., forming complete sentences, using 'I do not like', distinguishing colors).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) to ensure continued progress in expressing ideas about the garden? (e.g., providing sentence starters for writing, reviewing color names again).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and sentence structures practiced today to write simple sentences and short phrases about the garden, describing plants and their colors.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>